While investigating #15382, I discovered that the `pkg/cloudprovider/providers/gce/gce.go:waitForOp(...)` method (copied below) sometimes causes panics due to `invalid memory address or nil pointer dereference`.

``` GO
func waitForOp(op *compute.Operation, getOperation func() (*compute.Operation, error)) error {
    pollOp := op
    consecPollFails := 0
    for pollOp.Status != "DONE" {
        var err error
        time.Sleep(3 * time.Second)
        pollOp, err = getOperation()
        if err != nil {
            if consecPollFails == 2 {
                // Only bail if we've seen 3 consecutive polling errors.
                return err
            }
            consecPollFails++
        } else {
            consecPollFails = 0
        }
    }
    if pollOp.Error != nil && len(pollOp.Error.Errors) > 0 {
        return &googleapi.Error{
            Code:    int(pollOp.HttpErrorStatusCode),
            Message: pollOp.Error.Errors[0].Message,
        }
    }
    return nil

}
```

Error: `E1020 20:36:34.301927    3625 util.go:82] Recovered from panic: "invalid memory address or nil pointer dereference" (runtime error: invalid memory address or nil pointer dereference)`

Stack trace indicates the culprit is this line:

``` GO
    for pollOp.Status != "DONE" {
```

I'll fix this along with #15382. This method also has the potential to block indefinitely if there is a bad server response, so the fix should also fix the max amount of time to wait.

Reproduce steps:
1. Install a V1.1.0-alpha.1 cluster
2. Create a service with the following json file
{
   "kind":"Service",
   "apiVersion":"v1",
   "metadata":{
      "name":"sshd",
      "labels":{
         "name":"sshd"
      }  
   },  
   "spec":{
      "ports": [
        {  
            "port": 48000,
            "targetPort": 22, 
            "protocol": "TCP"
        }  
      ],  
      "selector":{
         "name":"sshd"
      },  
      "externalIPs": [
          "172.30.80.20"
      ]  
   }  
}
1. After the service is created, kube-proxy on node 172.30.80.20 listens on port 48000
   # netstat -anp | grep 48000

   tcp        0      0 172.30.80.20:48000      0.0.0.0:\*               LISTEN      54819/kube-proxy
2. Delete the service by kubectl.
3. After the service is deleted, the port 48000 on  node 172.30.80.20 is still there.
   # netstat -anp | grep 48000
   tcp        0      0 172.30.80.20:48000      0.0.0.0:\*               LISTEN      54819/kube-proxy

Right now the [cluster_upgrade e2e](https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/blob/master/test/e2e/cluster_upgrade.go) takes:

```
// realVersion turns a version constant s--one accepted by cluster/gce/upgrade.sh--
// into a deployable version string. If the s is not known to be a version
// constant, it will assume it is already a valid version, and return s directly.
//
// NOTE: KEEP THIS LIST UP-TO-DATE WITH THE CODE BELOW.
// The version strings supported are:
// - "latest_stable"   (returns a string like "0.18.2")
// - "latest_release"  (returns a string like "0.19.1")
// - "latest_ci"       (returns a string like "0.19.1-669-gabac8c8")
```

And the [upgrade.sh](https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/blob/master/cluster/gce/upgrade.sh) script takes similar params (via [set_binary_version](https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/blob/master/cluster/common.sh#L234):

```
${0} [-M|-N|-P] -l | <release or continuous integration version> | [latest_stable|latest_release|latest_ci]
```

Furthermore, there is the script [hack/get-build.sh](https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/blob/master/hack/get-build.sh), as documented in [Getting Kubernetes Builds](https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/blob/master/docs/devel/getting-builds.md).

These all need to take an arbitrary build string:
- `ci/latest`
- `ci/latest-1`
- `ci/latest-1.2`
- `release/latest`
- `release/latest-1`
- `release/latest-1.2`
- `release/stable`
- `release/stable-1`
- `release/stable-1.2`

So that we can test upgrades properly, and generally for functioning upgrades.

The logic should also be consolidated if possible.

