I have just had to re-write this loop:

``` go
    var got *api.Namespace
    for t := time.Now(); time.Since(t) < singleCallTimeout; time.Sleep(poll) {
        got, err = c.Namespaces().Create(namespaceObj)
        if err == nil {
            break
        }
    }
    if err != nil {
        return nil, err
    }
```

as:

``` go
    var got *api.Namespace
    if err := wait.Poll(poll, timeout, func() (bool, error) {
        var err error
        got, err = c.Namespaces().Create(namespaceObj)
        if err != nil {
            return false, nil
        }
        return true, nil
    }); err != nil {
        return nil, err
    }
```

because we are supposed to use `wait.Poll` in our tests.

I don't llike this. With the lack of generics in Go we can't do a good job of writing such a function. Ideally, we should paramaterize on result type of the operation so created values can be returned and so that any information required by the closure can be passed as an argument. We can't do this in Go today so in the closure we end up using a value from the outer scope (here `got`) which is horrible. I also don't like the use of a closure here (and the underlying implementation uses channels) -- all of this seems like a lot of complexity for what is a fairly simple procedure.

Personally, I find the `wait.Poll` pattern hard to understand and I think it makes our tests harder to write and maintain.

